# GBA native C toolchain (Smaller C ARM retarget) - design notes and log

DiscoBSD/GBA. Goal: on-target self-hosted `cc/as/ld` emitting the GBA
kernel's a.out. Sections 0-6 are the original survey of the seams to fill
for ARM (written before any code); sections 7 on log the work, in order.
Where a later section supersedes an earlier one, it says so.

## 0. The pipeline that existed in-tree (all BSD, originally MIPS only)

    cc (usr.bin/cc)  driver: cpp -> smlrc -> as -> ld
      usr.bin/cpp    preprocessor (Frunze)
      usr.bin/smlrc  Smaller C compiler  -> emits ASSEMBLY TEXT
      usr.bin/as     assembler (Vakulenko, MIPS32) -> a.out object (.o)
      usr.bin/ld     linker -> a.out OMAGIC executable

Key architectural fact confirmed: **smlrc emits assembly TEXT**, not object
code. So the ARM job splits cleanly in three:
  1. smlrc: add `cgarm.c` that emits ARM/Thumb *assembly text*.
  2. as: parse+encode ARM/Thumb text -> a.out object with ARM relocs.
  3. ld: resolve ARM relocs + ARM/Thumb interworking veneers -> OMAGIC.

## 1. a.out container (LOCKED — verified against the kernel loader)

Producer of the final executable = `usr.bin/ld`. It MUST match
`sys/kern/exec_aout.c` + `sys/sys/exec_aout.h`:
- `struct exec` = 8 LE 32-bit words: a_midmag, a_text, a_data, a_bss,
  a_reltext, a_reldata, a_syms, a_entry. GBA is little-endian: no swap.
- **OMAGIC (0407) ONLY** — the kernel loader's switch rejects everything else.
- **MID = 0 (MID_ZERO), flags = 0** — loader asserts N_GETMID==0 && N_GETFLAG==0.
  So a_midmag = 0x00000107. (NOT MID_ARM6.)
- Load/link the whole text+data blob at **__user_data_start = 0x02001800**
  (EWRAM), entry = a_entry = `_start`. bss after. No relocs/syms in the
  final executable (a_reltext=a_reldata=a_syms=0). File = 32B header + blob.
- Object files (as output / ld input): a.out header + BSD nlist
  {n_strx, n_type(N_UNDF/ABS/TEXT/DATA/BSS | N_EXT), n_ovly, n_value} +
  `struct reloc {flags, index, offset}` — all in tools/aoututils/include/
  {a.out.h,nlist.h}, which #include sys/exec_aout.h (shared container, no drift).
  `struct reloc` format nibble types today are MIPS (RWORD26, RHIGH16/S,
  RGPREL...). **ARM needs new format types** (added: RBYTE32 serves ABS32,
  RARM_PC24 - see sections 8-9): ABS32, ARM PC24 (BL/B),
  Thumb BL/BLX pair, optionally movw/movt (R_ARM_MOVW/MOVT_ABS).

## 2. smlrc backend contract (what cgarm.c must implement)

Target selected by a `-D` macro in usr.bin/smlrc/Makefile
(`-DMIPS` -> `#include "cgmips.c"`, `-DTR3200` -> cgx86.c). Add `-DARM` +
`#ifdef ARM #include "cgarm.c"` in smlrc.c near line 2259-2270, plus
`-D__arm__`. cgmips.c = 2264 lines; cgx86.c = 3408; budget cgarm.c similarly.

### 2a. PUBLIC seam — the entry points smlrc.c (shared core) calls
(from smlrc.c refs ~lines 390-436, 623, 7768). cgarm.c MUST provide all:

  GenInit, GenInitParams, GenInitFinalize
  GenStartCommentLine, GenWordAlignment(bss)
  GenLabel(label,static), GenNumLabel(n)
  GenZeroData(size,bss), GenIntData(size,val), GenAddrData(size,label,ofs),
    GenStartAsciiString, GenDumpChar(ch)
  GenExpr(), GenFin()
  GenFxnProlog, GenFxnEpilog, GenIsrProlog, GenIsrEpilog, GenMaxLocalsSize
  GenJumpUncond(label), GenJumpIfZero(label), GenJumpIfNotZero(label),
    GenJumpIfEqual(val,label)
  + flag GenExterns (emit .globl for non-static)

Everything else in cgmips.c is INTERNAL to the backend's own implementation
of the above (not called by the core): the instruction enum + GenPrintInstr,
the operand model (GenPrintOperand: reg / indirect-reg / const / label /
numlabel / label-lo), GenPrintInstr{1,2,3}Operands, the temp-register stack
(GenPushReg/GenPopReg, MAX_TEMP_REGS=8), GenExpr0 (the big expression walker,
~line 1533), GenPrep, GenCmp, GenReadIdent/Local/Indirect,
GenWriteIdent/Local/Indirect, GenIncDec*, GenPostIncDec*. These are the bulk
and get fully rewritten for ARM — this is Phase 1a, the multi-day part.

### 2b. GenInit knobs to set for ARM
SizeOfWord=4; OutputFormat=FormatSegmented; section headers
".text"/".data"/".rdata"/".bss"; UseLeadingUnderscores=0 (or match as);
FileHeader (MIPS uses ".set reorder"; ARM: probably ARM/Thumb mode directive
like ".code 16"/".thumb" or ".arm" depending on default emission).
.align 2 (word), .byte/.half/.word for data, .space for bss, .ascii for strings.

### 2c. Frame / calling convention model (MIPS today, to map to ARM)
MIPS frame (cgmips.c GenWriteFrameSize/GenFxnProlog/GenFxnEpilog, 522-598):
- SP=$29 grows down; FP=$30; RA=$31 saved at [FP+4], old FP at [FP+0].
- Prolog: `subu sp,sp,size; sw fp,size-8(sp); addu fp,sp,size-8; sw ra,4(fp)`
  (ra store suppressed for leaf fns). Args A0-A3 spilled to [sp+4*i].
- Frame size is BACK-PATCHED (GenUpdateFrameSize seeks with fsetpos to the
  prolog position once locals are known) — cgarm.c must keep the same
  fgetpos/fsetpos back-patch trick.
- Locals at negative offsets from FP (CurFxnMinLocalOfs).
- Temp-reg stack: T0..T7 hold subexpression results; T8/T9 scratch (like AT).
- Return value in V0.
ARM mapping (AAPCS-ish, keep it minimal/simple like Smaller C):
  args r0-r3 (+stack), return r0, temps r4-r10 (callee-saved -> save/restore
  in prolog/epilog), fp=r11, sp=r13, lr=r14, pc=r15. Leaf-fn optimization =
  don't push lr. Same back-patched frame size. BL for calls (sets GenLeaf=0),
  BX lr (or pop {..,pc}) for return, with ARM/Thumb interworking on calls.

## 3. as (usr.bin/as, ~2862 lines) ARM/Thumb work

**No -D arch seam** (unlike smlrc): MIPS knowledge is hardcoded, so retarget =
edit/fork the C. Two trees to touch: usr.bin/as (the TARGET a.out binary,
built via cross-gcc->ELF->elf2aout) and tools/aoututils/as (host copy used in
the cross build). Both build the same sources.

Pipeline (main as.c:2751): startup -> hashinit -> pass1 -> middle -> pass2 ->
makereloc -> makesymtab -> makeheader. Output order: header, text, data(+strng),
text-relocs, data-relocs, symbols.

REPLACE:
- **Opcode table** `struct optable {opcode; name; type; func}` (as.c:168-173) +
  the operand-format flag scheme (FRD*/FRT*/FOFF16/FHIGH16/FAOFF28... as.c:178-200)
  + the whole `optable[]` (as.c:209-333). These flags encode both which operands
  to parse AND where they land in the 32-bit word = deeply MIPS-shaped.
- Operand parse/field-pack in `makecmd` (1336-1756). DELETE the delay-slot /
  FDSLOT / reorder machinery and the MIPS li/la macro expanders (emit_li 1270,
  emit_la 1303) - no ARM analog.
- The in-assembler fixup engine `relocate` (2477-2512): a SECOND copy of the
  6 MIPS reloc formats to rewrite for ARM (mirrors ld's).
- `lookreg` (802) register-name table -> ARM regs.
KEEP (arch-neutral, works as-is): lexer getlex(976), expr parser
getterm/getexpr(1067/1137), symtab lookname(925)/hashing, sections .text/.data/
.bss/.rdata (setsection 772), labels incl. numeric 1f/1b (findlabel 2393), data
dirs .word(2057)/.byte/.half/.space/.align(align 1938)/.ascii(makeascii 1783),
.globl/.local/.weak/.comm(2126-2224), the many ignored GNU dirs (.ent/.type/...).
On-disk records: nlist = n_len(1B) n_type(1B) n_value(4B) name-bytes (fputsym 505);
reloc = variable 1-6B: flags(1B) [+index(3B) if REXT] [+offset(2B) if RHIGH16/S]
(fputrel 463). Header `makeheader` (2457): a_midmag=RMAGIC bare (MID=0), a_entry=0.
NEW reloc format types needed in the 3-bit RFMASK field (a.out.h:54-60, only 6/8
used today): ABS32, ARM PC24 (BL/B), Thumb BL/BLX pair, movw/movt lo/hi.

## 4. ld (usr.bin/ld, ~1571 lines) ARM/Thumb work

Same two-tree note (usr.bin/ld + tools/aoututils/ld). Passes (main ld.c:1510):
pass1(scan/resolve) -> middle(assign addrs) -> setupout -> pass2(relocate/emit)
-> finishout.

REPLACE:
- **`relword` (ld.c:524-645)** - THE reloc-apply switch. Two sub-switches on
  rel->flags&RFMASK: extract (535-559) then patch-back (605-637). Rewrite the
  MIPS formats -> ARM: RBYTE32(611 `word=addr+delta`) is the nearest analog to
  ARM ABS32; RWORD26(622, MIPS J/JAL >>2 26-bit) is where ARM PC24 BL / Thumb
  BL/BLX-pair goes but the field width, >>2 word-scaling, and MIPS implicit-top-
  4-PC-bits differ FUNDAMENTALLY from ARM signed PC-relative BL; RHIGH16/S
  (627/632, MIPS lui) -> ARM movw/movt split-immediate (scattered bitfields, not
  contiguous word&0xffff).
- **ADD the entire veneer subsystem - currently ABSENT.** ld does NO stub /
  veneer / long-branch island / Thumb-interworking / BLX / range-checking; it
  assumes every reloc fits in place. A GBA arm7tdmi thumb-interwork target MUST
  add: BL-range checks, veneer/island synthesis, Thumb-bit (BLX) mode-change.
  This is the single biggest gap (and historically delicate here: a broken
  Thumb->ARM veneer once crashed a whole cross-built link).  ld_arm still has
  no veneers; it links all-ARM code (section 9).
- **`BADDR 0x7f008000` (ld.c:52)** the MIPS load base -> GBA **0x02001800**
  (our verified __user_data_start); overridable with `-T`. Drop the MIPS
  `_gp`/`gpoffset=0x8000`(137)/RGPREL small-data path (dead on ARM).
Header `finishout` (1489): a_midmag = RMAGIC(-r) / **OMAGIC** (final exe), MID=0,
a_entry = entrypt->n_value (needs N_EXT+N_TEXT, our `_start`) else basaddr.
Final resolved link emits NO relocs/syms unless -r/-s (OMAGIC blob) = exactly
the kernel-loadable form.

### !! CORRECTION to the survey's advice
The survey suggested "start setting MID_ARM6 (143)" in as/ld headers. **DO NOT.**
The GBA kernel loader (exec_aout.c) asserts `N_GETMID==MID_ZERO && N_GETFLAG==0`
and REJECTS anything else. Keep the existing bare RMAGIC/OMAGIC with MID=0 and
flags=0. Only the load base (0x02001800) and the ARM reloc/veneer logic change.

## 5. Bootstrap plan (cross-then-native)
- Phase 1 (host cross): build cc/smlrc/cpp/as/ld as host tools for ARM.
  1a cgarm.c (ARM-only first, then +Thumb/BX). 1b as ARM/Thumb+relocs.
  1c ld ARM relocs+veneers. Test: tiny C -> ARM a.out -> mGBA under
  DiscoBSD/GBA; diff vs arm-elf-gcc+elf2aout.
- Phase 2 (native): cross-build the ARM cc set itself into a.out, install to
  the GBA root /usr/{bin,libexec}, run `cc hello.c` on-target. Needs the
  out-of-swap->kill kernel change (done: the scheduler SIGKILLs) so a
  too-big compile fails gracefully instead of panicking.
- Phase 3: build libc.a in ARM a.out (tools/aoututils host or native as/ld).

## 6. Risks
(1) ld ARM/Thumb interworking veneers (historically delicate here).
(2) Smaller C is a C89-ish subset — first targets = small programs.
(3) on-target RAM for large compiles.

## 7. Phase 1a status: cgarm.c DONE and execution-validated (2026-09-15)

usr.bin/smlrc/cgarm.c written; usr.bin/smlrc/smlrc.c wired for `-DARM`
(NO_STRUCT_BY_VAL + NO_FP forced for ARM; include-ladder branch added;
CAN_COMPILE_32BIT auto-set on a host build). MIPS/x86 builds verified
UNCHANGED (the ARM bits are all `#ifdef ARM`).

Target = **ARMv4T / ARM7TDMI** (GBA): NO movw/movt (big constants -> `ldr
rN,=imm` literal pools, flushed with a per-function `.ltorg` in the epilog),
NO `blx <reg>` (indirect call = `mov lr,pc; bx rN`), NO hardware divide
(`/ % u/ u%` -> `__aeabi_idiv/uidiv/idivmod/uidivmod` runtime calls).

Emits GNU-as ARM (`.syntax unified` / `.arm`). Register model: r0 = working/
return reg (the core's V0) AND arg0 (they coincide on ARM, unlike MIPS $2/$4);
r0-r3 = args; r4-r10 = the 7-deep temp-register stack (T0..T6); ip(r12) =
TEMP_REG_A, lr(r14) = TEMP_REG_B (momentary scratch, never live across a bl);
r11 = frame pointer.

Frame (GenFxnProlog/Epilog) realizes the core's convention (AddFxnParamSymbols
puts param1 at fp+2*SizeOfWord) AND is AAPCS-compatible:
  sub sp,#24; str {r11,lr,r0,r1,r2,r3} at [sp+0,4,8,12,16,20]; mov r11,sp;
  push {r4-r10}; ldr ip,=<locals>; sub sp,sp,ip
  => [fp+0]=oldfp [fp+4]=retaddr [fp+8..20]=param regs [fp+24..]=stack params
     fp = entry_sp-24, locals at fp-negative. Epilog restores r4-r11/sp, bx lr.
  (Superseded by section 13: the saved r4-r10 overlapped the locals; the
  prolog now saves only the registers used, below the locals.)
The `<locals>` word is emitted fixed-width (`=0x%08X`) so the single-pass
prolog is back-patched in place via fgetpos/fsetpos (same trick as cgmips).

Comparisons REWRITTEN ARM-idiomatic (flags: cmp + b<cc> / mov;mov<cc> #1) - the
whole MIPS slt/CmpBlocks machinery is gone. GenPrep copied verbatim (arch-
neutral). GenExpr0 ported from cgmips with ARM emit helpers.

KEY BUG FOUND+FIXED: division is a hidden `bl` that clobbers r0-r3, but the
temp-register model keeps live subexpression values in r0 - so an expression
containing `/ %` must NOT use the register-temp path. GenExpr0 now sets
CanUseTempRegs = (maxCallDepth==0 && no div/mod token) - spills to stack
instead. (MIPS is immune; its div isn't a call.)

Calls (increment 2): direct `bl`, recursive, and indirect (function pointer,
`mov lr,pc; bx ip`) all work for **<=4 args** (loaded into r0-r3 from the
pushed arg area, arg1->r0 = AAPCS-correct, so it interoperates with the
gcc-built libc for <=4-arg calls). >4 args -> errorInternal(200) for now
(since supported: the 5th and later args go on the stack, AAPCS order).

VALIDATION HARNESS (no qemu/sudo here): host-build smlrc with `gcc -DARM ...`;
compile C -> ARM asm -> arm-none-eabi-as -march=armv4t -> arm-none-eabi-ld
(pulls libgcc for __aeabi_*, and auto-inserts the ARM->Thumb interworking
veneers = a live preview of the Phase-1c ld work) -> load into the **Unicorn**
CPU emulator (pip --user unicorn), set r0-r3, run, check r0. 48 tests pass:
arithmetic/mul/div/mod (signed+unsigned), all comparisons, all shifts, if/else/
while/for, &&/||, bitwise, unary, arrays (local+global), globals, pointer args
(&local through a call), char/short sign+zero extension, big immediates,
params, recursion (fact/fib/gcd/sum), function pointers.

Deferred (documented limits, NOT bugs): struct-by-value, float (both #ifdef'd
off), >4 args (since added), __interrupt functions
(GenIsrProlog=errorInternal(201)), stack 8-byte alignment for
variadic/double libc calls.

cgarm.c committed 839dd10.

## 8. Phase 1b status: ARM assembler core DONE + validated (2026-09-15)

Prototype lives in usr.bin/as/arm-dev/ (asarm.c + Unicorn harnesses; a
development prototype, NOT yet wired into the build - see its README).
(Later integrated as usr.bin/as/as_arm.c, section 9.)

asarm.c is a from-scratch ARMv4T assembler for the EXACT assembly subset
cgarm.c emits (it was cleaner than retrofitting the MIPS as's deeply
MIPS-shaped operand-flag table). It reuses nothing from usr.bin/as yet; the
plan is to fold the validated encoder into usr.bin/as (+ tools/aoututils/as)
alongside the a.out object framing (which IS reused - see below).

DONE + validated:
- ARM instruction ENCODER: `asarm -e "<instr>"` prints the 32-bit encoding.
  Byte-exact vs `arm-none-eabi-as -march=armv4t` for all 42 instruction forms
  cgarm emits (mov/mvn/movCC, add/sub/rsb/and/orr/eor/bic +imm/+reg, cmp/cmn,
  lsl/lsr/asr imm+reg, mul, push/pop reglist incl. the single-reg str/ldr
  form, ldr/str/ldrb/strb/ldrh/strh/ldrsb/ldrsh with [rn,#imm]/pre!/post,
  b/bCC/bl, bx, mov lr,pc). Immediate encoding uses the 8-bit-rotated form.
- Two-pass file ASSEMBLER: `asarm in.s out.bin`. Labels (name: and .Lnnn:),
  sections (.text/.data/.section .rodata/.bss), data dirs (.word/.short/.byte/
  .space/.ascii/.align), .globl, and the `ldr rd,=imm|=sym` literal-pool
  pseudo flushed at `.ltorg` (pc-relative `ldr rd,[pc,#off]` + pooled .word).
  Produces a flat text+rodata+data image with internal labels + pools resolved.
- Functional: smlrc-arm -> asarm -> flat image -> Unicorn = 22/22 external-free
  programs correct (recursion fact/fib/sumc, calls, if/while/for, arrays, big
  immediates, char/short, comparisons). External-free because linking to
  libgcc (__aeabi_*) needs ld (Phase 1c).
  KEY BUG fixed: literal-pool bookkeeping must be driven by a per-pass running
  ldr-index (curldr), not the pass-1-only total (npool), or pass 2 never
  reserves/emits the pool word and the next function overwrites its slot.

DONE (Phase 1b increment 2): a.out OBJECT output - `asarm -c in.s out.o`.
Header a_midmag=RMAGIC(0406, MID=0) a_entry=0; then text, data(=.data ++
.rodata), text-relocs, data-relocs, symtab. Relocs are PARALLEL-PER-WORD (one
record per 4-byte word, in order; RABS=0x00 for a word needing none - matches
usr.bin/as makereloc's `for i+=WORDSZ`). Record = flags byte (RSMASK seg |
RFMASK fmt) [+3-byte index if REXT]. nlist = n_len(1) n_type(1) n_value(4 LE)
name; symbol n_value is SEGMENT-RELATIVE, n_type = N_TEXT/N_DATA/N_BSS|N_EXT.
ARM reloc types: RBYTE32=0x01 (ABS32) for `.word sym`/pool `=sym` (internal =
segment reloc RSEG_TEXT/DATA/BSS, external = RSEG_EXT+index); new RARM_PC24=
0x06 for `bl <external>` (internal bl is PC-relative = resolved, no reloc).
KEY BUG fixed: an external symbol referenced only by a reloc must be forced
into the symtab (asarm's addrel now calls sym_get) or its reloc gets index 0.

## 9. Phase 1c: ldarm (linker) DONE + full self-hosted chain validated

usr.bin/as/arm-dev/ldarm.c - reads asarm's a.out objects, concatenates
text/data/bss, builds a global symbol table, applies ARM relocations (ABS32
= add final segment/symbol base; PC24 = (target-(P+8))>>2 into the low 24
bits), writes a flat linked image + prints ENTRY/SYM. Thumb interworking
veneers not needed yet (all-ARM); that is the remaining piece for a Thumb libc.
  ldarm -Ttext=<hex> -e <entry> -o out.bin obj1.o obj2.o ...

FULL CHAIN VALIDATED (smlrc-arm -> asarm -c -> ldarm -> Unicorn, NO GNU tools
in the chain): cross-object calls (external PC24), bss globals (ABS32),
division through a self-hosted __aeabi_uidiv/idiv runtime written in C
(repeated subtraction, no '/'), recursion (fact/fib), loops, arrays, and a
`mainall` that sums 8 such functions - all correct. Plus the 42/42 encoder and
22/22 single-object functional tests from section 8.

INTEGRATED into the tree (2026-09-16): the ARM assembler and linker now live at
usr.bin/as/as_arm.c and usr.bin/ld/ld_arm.c and are built for MACHINE_ARCH=arm
(the Makefiles pick as_arm.o/ld_arm.o via a shell `!=` select that works under
both BSD make and GNU make; mips keeps as.c/ld.c). RARM_PC24=0x06 added to
include/a.out.h and tools/aoututils/include/a.out.h. Both sources compile clean
via the tree's arm7tdmi toolchain; the full self-hosted chain (9/9) re-verified
through the integrated sources. CLI: `as_arm -o out.o in.s` (object; -b flat,
-e encode), `ld_arm -Ttext=<hex> -e <entry> -o out obj...`. Dev harnesses moved
to usr.bin/as/arm-dev/ (build.sh builds them from the tree sources).

## 10. cc driver wired for ARM (2026-09-16)

The driver usr.bin/cc/cc.c ALREADY drives the Smaller C toolchain for every
arch: invoked as `cc`, its default mode is MODE_SMALLERC -> pass0 =
/usr/libexec/smlrc, and it calls the preprocessor (/usr/bin/cpp) and `as`/`ld`
by name (which resolve to as_arm/ld_arm on arm). The `#ifdef MULTITARGET`
ccom_<mach> override (cc.c:828) is NOT compiled - the cc Makefile never defines
MULTITARGET - so nothing clobbers the smlrc default. So the pipeline
cc -> cpp -> smlrc -> as -> ld is arch-neutral and needed NO driver code change.

The one gap was smlrc's own build: usr.bin/smlrc/Makefile hard-coded -DMIPS and
-mips16. Fixed to select the code generator by MACHINE_ARCH (shell `!=`, both
makes): arm -> -DARM (cgarm.c), no -mips16; mips -> -DMIPS -mips16. smlrc.o now
compiles for arm via the tree's arm7tdmi toolchain. (cgarm.c also lost two
unused functions; the 3 remaining warnings are pre-existing smlrc.c
vprintf/vfprintf/vsprintf builtin-mismatch, unrelated.)

VERIFIED end to end: the exact driver pipeline run by hand on a program using
#define macros - `cpp -P hello.c | smlrc-arm | as_arm -o | ld_arm` - links and
returns the right value in Unicorn (70 for `(4+3)*10`).

## 11. ARM crt0 + minimal libc + OMAGIC executables (2026-09-16)

Native ARM (a.out) runtime in usr.bin/as/arm-dev/rt/ (crt0.s, clib.s), built by
smlrc -> as_arm -> ld_arm. crt0.s: _start (environ=envp; main(argc,argv,envp);
_exit) - ARM, even entry so ISA-aware exec enters ARM. clib.s: syscalls via the
trampoline vector (then 0x03006400, now SYSCALL_VECTOR_ADDR = 0x03007E00)
(write/read/open/close/lseek/_exit/exit/_brk), sbrk
(_end/_curbrk), errno, and __aeabi_uidiv/idiv/uidivmod/idivmod (repeated
subtraction - correct, slow; uses only instructions as_arm emits, so no
flag-setting S-suffix or tst needed).

ld_arm now emits a DiscoBSD **OMAGIC a.out executable by default**: 8-word
header a_midmag=0x00000107 (OMAGIC 0407 | MID 0), a_text/a_data/a_bss,
a_reltext=a_reldata=a_syms=0, a_entry=_start; text+data follow (bss implied);
default load base 0x02001800 (override -T; -b = old flat image for Unicorn).
Added linker symbols _end/_edata/_etext (+ no-underscore aliases). Verified the
header (magic 0407, MID 0, entry EVEN=ARM, size=32+text+data).

BUG FOUND+FIXED in cgarm.c (committed backend): label references in
instructions used raw "=%s" so a string literal compiled to `ldr r0, =3`
(the integer 3) instead of `ldr r0, =.L3`. Added GenLdrEq() routing through
GenPrintLabel (".L" prefix for numeric labels); GenReadIdent/GenWriteIdent/
tokIdent now use it. cgarm regression still 9/9.

VALIDATED end to end in Unicorn with an emulated 0x03006400 trampoline: hello.c
(write a string) and num.c (print numbers via /,%,recursion,char,&local) both
run and exit 0, BOTH as a flat image (rt/run_rt.py) AND as an OMAGIC a.out
loaded exactly like the kernel does - at 0x02001800, ARM entry, user stack with
argc/argv/envp, syscall vector (rt/run_omagic.py). So `cc hello.c` now yields a
GBA-loadable a.out that enters ARM and uses real syscalls.

REMAINING for a complete Phase 1 (at the time; since done unless noted -
sections 12-13: on-hardware runs, stdio/printf/malloc, ld_arm -l/-L and
archives, __aeabi_uidivmod/idivmod in clib.s, >4 args, Phase 2.  Still open:
Thumb veneers in ld, struct-by-value, float):
- On-hardware test: drop a built .out into the userland SD/ROM image and run it
  from the shell (needs the ISA-aware-exec kernel 0e9de6f). A proper lib
  Makefile to build crt0.o + libc.a with the native tools. More libc (printf,
  string, malloc) as smlrc coverage grows. ld_arm -l/-L/archive handling.
- tools/aoututils/{as,ld} host builds for arm (optional; the arm build uses
  arm-none-eabi today, so host ARM as/ld are only needed to self-host).
- Testing the actual cc.c binary on host is awkward (absolute /usr paths); it
  is validated by inspection + the by-hand pipeline above. On-target it runs in
  Phase 2.
- Thumb interworking veneers in ld (to link the Thumb-built libc), OR build an
  ARM libc.
- __aeabi_idivmod/uidivmod (r0=quot, r1=rem) for '%' - needs a tiny asm runtime
  (a C function can't return two registers); asarm would need numeric local
  labels (1f/1b) or use .L labels.
- smlrc side: >4 args, struct-by-value, float.
- Phase 2: cross-build cc/as/ld themselves into GBA a.out and run on-target.

Reproduce: pip install --user --break-system-packages unicorn; build smlrc-arm
(NOTES 7), asarm (`gcc -O1 -o asarm asarm.c`), ldarm (`gcc -O1 -o ldarm
ldarm.c`); harnesses run.py/run_asarm.py/run_ld.py in usr.bin/as/arm-dev/.

## 12. Toward self-hosting smlrc: native stdio + streaming as_arm (2026-10-19)

Goal: compile smlrc.c ON the GBA with its own toolchain.  smlrc itself
already streams (one pass, output written as it goes, fgetpos/fsetpos to
back-patch frame sizes) and fits the 249 KB window.  This step makes the
native libc able to run it, and as_arm able to assemble it.

Native libc (usr.bin/as/arm-dev/rt): new stdio.c.  FILE has the same
layout as struct _iobuf in the shipped <stdio.h>, with _iob[], _filbuf
and _flsbuf, so the header's getc/putc/putchar macros and stdin/stdout/
stderr work too.  fopen/fclose/fgetc/fputc/fputs/fflush/fseek/ftell/
fgetpos/fsetpos, buffered files (BUFSIZ 1024, malloc'd per slot),
unbuffered stdout/stderr, exit() flushes (crt0 now calls exit, not _exit).
printf family (printf/fprintf/sprintf/vfprintf/vprintf/vsprintf) with
flags - 0 + space #, width/precision (and *), l/h, and d i u o x X c s p %
(%p as the cross libc: "(nil)" or 0x-hex).  ctype functions (isalpha,
isdigit, isalnum, isspace, ... over the existing _ctype_ table).  smlrc-arm
is built NO_PREPROCESSOR, so rt/Makefile runs stdio.c through the host cpp.
`make test` in rt builds stdiotest.c natively and with the host compiler,
runs the native one in Unicorn (rt/run_file.py: OMAGIC loader in the
GBAED window, file syscalls on host files) and compares output and files.

as_arm bugs found on the way (both affect any large program):
- enc_imm8r() wrote the rotate field as 16-i instead of i, so every
  rotated immediate (outside 0..255) was wrong except rot 8: e.g.
  `mov r0,#0x80000000` gave 8, `mvn r4,#-2147483648` (= 0x7fffffff) gave
  a small value.  Now matches arm-none-eabi-as.
- `ldr rd,=x` masked an out-of-range pool offset with & 0xFFF.  cgarm
  emits .ltorg only at function end, so functions over 4 KB (smlrc has 5,
  exprval is 23 KB) loaded garbage (457 such loads in smlrc).  as_arm now
  places literal-pool islands after an unconditional `b` when the oldest
  pending literal is over 1 KB back (same decision in both passes), and
  fails with a clear error if a literal is still out of reach.

as_arm streaming rewrite: input read twice (no line array); section bytes
written in order straight to their file offset (sizes known after pass 1);
text relocation records streamed to <out>.rtmp with external symbols
renumbered when copied in; data relocations, one literal group and the
symbol table stay in memory (.L<n> labels in a numeric table, other names
hashed).  No fixed limits on lines/symbols/relocs/section size.  Output is
byte-identical to the previous as_arm (with the two fixes) for smlrc.s,
the rt sources and tests, in object and flat (-b) mode.  Device `as`:
bss 184.5 KB -> 13.6 KB; peak heap for smlrc.s about 40 KB.

Validated in Unicorn (host): native smlrc (smlrc.c compiled by smlrc-arm,
assembled, linked with crt0 + the new libc.a; 177 KB in the 249 KB window)
compiles stdio.c and smlrc.c itself to output byte-identical to the host
smlrc-arm (smlrc.c: 38,155 lines, ~32 s in Unicorn).  The cross-built
device `as` assembles smlrc.s identically under run_file.py.
On real GBAED hardware the new libc and streaming as build and run
hello.c and ctest.c through the on-device cc.

ld_arm streaming rewrite: pass 1 reads only headers and symbol tables
(archive members are pulled in the same order as before); pass 2 reads
each segment and its relocation records side by side through two file
handles, a word at a time, and writes the relocated words straight out.
Global symbols are hashed with variable-length names; each object keeps
its sizes and a symbol-number -> global map.  Output is byte-identical to
the previous ld_arm (hello, stdiotest, smlrc; a.out and -b).  Device ld:
bss 78.7 KB -> 2.2 KB.

Self-host rehearsal in Unicorn with the DEVICE binaries (run_file.py):
device cpp + smlrc + as + ld build smlrc.c into smlrc1 (151,676 bytes);
smlrc1 compiles smlrc.c to the same .s; as + ld turn that into smlrc2,
byte-identical to smlrc1 - a fixed point.  The defines smlrc.c needs come
on the command line (cc passes -D through to cpp):
  -D__SMALLER_C_32__ -D__SMALLER_C_SCHAR__ -DARM -D_RETROBSD
  -DNO_ANNOTATIONS -DNO_PREPROCESSOR -DNO_PPACK -DNO_EXTRA_WARNS -DSTATIC
  -DSYNTAX_STACK_MAX=3200   (smlrc.c #includes cgarm.c from its directory)

On the device: share/examples/gba/selfhost/selfhost.sh, installed in
/usr/share/examples/gba/selfhost with copies of smlrc.c and cgarm.c, runs
the stages by hand (cpp, smlrc, as, ld; not cc, so stage 2 can use the new
compiler) and cmp's smlrc1 against smlrc2.

SELF-HOSTED ON REAL GBAED HARDWARE (2026-10-19/20): selfhost.sh printed
"smlrc1 and smlrc2 are identical".  Stage 1 (the cross-built smlrc, gcc
-Os) took 3 min 41 s.  Stage 2 (smlrc1, built by Smaller C itself:
unoptimized, division by repeated subtraction) took 1 h 23 min, about 20x
slower, close to the 15x seen in Unicorn.  One "sd: close: card-ready wait
timed out" warning appeared during stage 2 without affecting the result.
(That warning was the sd_close_rw() post-CMD12 wait; fixed in 98daf379.)

Division speed-up (2026-10-20): clib.s's __aeabi_uidivmod subtracted the
divisor once per unit of quotient (e.g. 0xffffffff/1 = 4 billion steps),
which dominated smlrc1's run time.  It is now shift-and-subtract (align
the divisor, then walk it back down: at most 32+32 steps), using only
instructions as_arm emits (cond-suffixed sub/orr, cmp/blt for the top
bit; no S suffix or tst).  rt/divtest.c, run by `make test`, checks
signed and unsigned / and % over edge-value pairs against the host.  In
Unicorn smlrc1 now compiles smlrc.c in 11.7 s instead of 32 s (2.7x),
with the same output.
On real GBAED, selfhost.sh stage 2 dropped from 1 h 23 min to 7 min
50 s (10.6x), still a fixed point.  Stage 1 (cross-built smlrc) is
unchanged at 3 min 43 s.

## 13. Speeding up the self-built compiler (2026-10-20)

rt/run_file.py --profile FILE writes per-basic-block run counts and
sizes.  Profiling smlrc1 compiling smlrc.c (459 M instructions) showed
most of the time in the native libc, which smlrc also compiles: strcmp
21%, memmove 6%, fputc 6%, strchr 3%, and the printf engine's per-char
helper calls.

- clib.s now has hand-written strcmp (compares unsigned char), strlen,
  strchr, memmove/memcpy (word copies when aligned, overlap-safe both
  ways), and the getc/putc fast paths fgetc/fputc (tail-calling
  _filbuf/_flsbuf).  The C versions are gone.  rt/strtest.c (in `make
  test`) checks them against the host over all alignments, lengths and
  overlaps.
- _doprnt calls fputc directly and counts its output, instead of a C
  helper (a full smlrc frame) per character.
Together: 459 M -> 306 M instructions (-33%).

cgarm prolog/epilog: the prolog always pushed r4-r10 just below fp, but
the locals also start at fp-4, so the first locals overwrote the saved
registers (harmless only because cgarm never keeps a temp live across a
call; any AAPCS caller expecting r4-r10 preserved would break).  Now prn()
records which of r4-r10 a function names, and the prolog is a fixed-width
placeholder back-patched at the epilog with the locals allocation
(immediate when encodable, none when empty) followed by a push of exactly
those registers, below the locals; the epilog pops them from there.
Same output semantics; smlrc still reaches its fixed point.  Unicorn time
for smlrc1 on smlrc.c: 11.7 s (after the division fix) -> 5.2 s with all
of the above.  On real GBAED, selfhost.sh stage 2 dropped from 7 min 50 s
to 5 min 39 s (still "smlrc1 and smlrc2 are identical"); 1 h 23 min
before the division fix.

Measured remaining waste (share of executed instructions): materializing
a comparison as 0/1 and re-testing it (&&/|| chains) ~5%, literal-pool
address loads for globals 5%, store-then-reload of a local 1.3%, and
locals/params living in memory (frame loads/stores ~25%) - the last needs
register allocation, a large change for a one-pass compiler.
